   "id": "taanit.17a.2",
   "daf": "17a",
   "sentenceId": 1,
   "he": "עַל הָרִאשׁוֹנָה הוּא אוֹמֵר: מִי שֶׁעָנָה אֶת אַבְרָהָם כּוּ׳. תָּנָא: יֵשׁ מַחְלִיפִין צְעָקָה לְאֵלִיָּהוּ, וּתְפִלָּה לִשְׁמוּאֵל. בִּשְׁלָמָא גַּבֵּי שְׁמוּאֵל — כְּתִיב בֵּיהּ תְּפִלָּה וּכְתִיב בֵּיהּ צְעָקָה.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «For the first» blessing «he recites: He Who answered Abraham.» It was «taught» in a baraita: «Some reverse» the order of the conclusion of two blessings, by reciting: Who hears «cries,» in the fifth blessing, which deals «with Elijah,» and the conclusion: Who hears «prayer,» in the blessing «of Samuel.» The Gemara asks: «Granted, with regard to Samuel» both options are appropriate, as «it is written concerning him: Prayer, and it is» likewise «written concerning him: Crying.» One verse states: “And Samuel said: Gather all of Israel to Mizpah, and I will pray for you to the Lord” (I Samuel 7:5), while another verse states: “And Samuel cried to the Lord for Israel” (I Samuel 7:9)."
  }
 ],
